…ts, you should still claim to get the other advantages. You get National Insurance credits automatically if your child is under 12 (useful if you're not working or don't earn enough to pay NI); if you don't need them, your husband/wife/partner can apply to transfer the credits, or a different family member providing care can apply for Specified Adult Childcare credits. You must report any change of circumstances to the Child Benefit Office.

HOW TO CLAIM — 'Make a claim' (gov.uk/child-benefit/how-to-claim): You can claim 48 hours after registering the birth, or once a child comes to live with you; claims can be backdated up to 3 months from the claim date. Decide who should claim (only one person per child; the claimant gets the NI credits towards state pension; if you live together only one of you can claim the higher rate for the eldest child). If under 16, you can claim yourself or someone responsible can claim on your behalf. Claim online via the 'Start now' service (also to add a child to an existing claim), or by post or phone if you cannot apply online. Before you start you'll need: your child's birth or adoption certificate (if you have it), your bank/building society details, your National Insurance number (if you have one), and your partner's National Insurance number (if applicable). If you don't have the certificate you can still claim (takes longer; may be asked to send it as proof; if not sent when asked you may need to restart). If the birth was registered outside the UK you'll need the original birth/adoption certificate and the child's passport or travel document used to enter the UK (eVisa details are checked, no share code needed); documents are usually returned within 4 weeks. You may also be able to manage someone else's Child Benefit claim.

NOTE: Per instructions I did not sign in, create an account, pay or enter personal data — I stopped at the informational pages and did not proceed into the online claim service (the 'Start now' link)."}
